563:
(a) Under O.C.G.A. § 48-5-290(d), no individual shall be appointed or reappointed to a county board of tax assessors when the individual is related to a member of the board of commissioners in one of the following degrees:
(1) Mother or mother-in-law;
(2) Father or father-in-law;
(3) Sister or sister-in-law;
(4) Brother or brother-in-law;
(5) Grandmother or grandmother by marriage;
(6) Grandfather or grandfather by marriage;
(7) Son or son-in-law; or
(8) Daughter or daughter-in-law.
(b) No individual shall serve as a member of the board of tax assessors:
(1) Who is less than 21 years of age.
(2) Who fails to make his residence within the county within six months after taking the oath of office as a member of the board.
(3) Who does not hold a high school diploma or its equivalent (an individual who has held an equivalent responsible position of employment for a period of five years shall not be required to meet the high school education requirement provided in this subsection).
(4) Who does not have at least one year's experience in appraisal-related work (the successful completion of 40 hours of approved appraisal courses provided for in O.C.G.A. § 48-5-291(c) taken either prior to appointment or within one year after appointment, may be substituted for this experience requirement).
(5) Who has not successfully completed an assessor examination to be administered by the commissioner or who has not successfully completed at least 40 hours of approved appraisal courses as provided in O.C.G.A. § 48-5-291(c).
(c) In addition, no member of the board of tax assessors shall be eligible to hold any state, county or municipal office during the time he holds office as a member of the board of tax assessors.
(Res. of 12-30-96, § II)

591:
(1) Purposes. The purposes of this article are to:
(a) Protect the public health, safety and well being of the citizens of the county;
(b) Collect and maintain information concerning the number, types and locations of businesses, professions, practitioners and occupations operating within the unincorporated areas of the county;
(c) Classify and categorize businesses, professions, practitioners and occupations operating within the unincorporated areas of the county according to the number of employees employed by each;
(d) Impose and collect an occupation tax on each business, profession, practitioner and occupation operating within the unincorporated areas of the county as authorized by the provisions of O.C.G.A. § 48-13-6 et seq.;
(e) Impose and collect a regulatory fee on certain businesses, professions, practitioners and occupations operating within the unincorporated areas of the county as authorized by the provisions of O.C.G.A. § 48-13-8 et seq.; and
(f) Provide for uniform rules, regulations, methods and exemptions for the collection and administration of the occupation tax and regulatory fees authorized hereunder.
(2) Findings. In connection with the adoption of this article, it is hereby ascertained, determined and declared by the board that:
(a) To ensure that the health, safety and welfare of the citizens of the county are protected, information must be collected and maintained concerning the number, types and locations of businesses, professions, practitioners and occupations operating within the unincorporated areas of the county;
(b) Uniform rules and regulations concerning the imposition, collection and administration of occupation taxes and regulatory fees are needed to carry out the purposes of this article; and
(c) The board has determined that categorizing businesses, professions, practitioners and occupations operating within the unincorporated areas of the county based upon number of employees and collection and imposition [of] an occupation tax and/or regulatory fee based upon said categorization is the most efficient, economic and beneficial means to carry out the purposes of this article.
(Ord. No. 09-006, § I, 7-7-09)

595:
(1) Applicability. Pursuant to the authority granted in O.C.G.A. § 48-13-8(a), there is hereby imposed a regulatory fee upon the following categories of businesses, professions, practitioners and occupations transacting business within the unincorporated areas of the county:
(a) Building and construction contractors, subcontractors, and workers;
(b) Carnivals;
(c) Taxicab and limousine operators;
(d) Tattoo artists;
(e) Stables;
(f) Shooting galleries and firearm ranges;
(g) Scrap metal processors;
(h) Pawnbrokers;
(i) Food service establishments;
(j) Dealers in precious metals;
(k) Firearms dealers;
(l) Peddlers;
(m) Parking lots;
(n) Nursing and personal care homes;
(o) Newspaper vending boxes;
(p) Modeling agencies;
(q) Massage parlors;
(r) Landfills;
(s) Auto and motorcycle racing;
(t) Boarding houses;
(u) Businesses which provide appearance bonds;
(v) Boxing and wrestling promoters;
(w) Hotels and motels;
(x) Hypnotists;
(y) Handwriting analysts;
(z) Health clubs, gyms, and spas;
(aa) Fortunetellers;
(bb) Garbage collectors;
(cc) Escort services;
(dd) Burglar and fire alarm installers;
(ee) Locksmiths; and
(ff) Any type of business, profession, practitioner or occupation authorized under the provisions of O.C.G.A. § 48-13-9(a) and (b).
The county manager (or such other county officer as designated by the board from time to time) shall notify any business, profession, practitioner or occupation required to pay a regulatory fee.
(2) Purpose and scope of regulatory fee. The regulatory fee collected pursuant to this section is collected from businesses, professions, practitioners and occupations for which the county customarily performs investigation or inspection in order to protect the public health, safety, or welfare of the citizens of the county or in the course of enforcing a state or local building, health, or safety code. The regulatory fee is not collected or used as a means of raising revenue for general purposes but is collected to offset the reasonable cost of the actual regulatory activity performed by the county.
(3) Collection and payment of regulatory fees.
(a) The regulatory fee required hereunder shall be due in the amount and according to the schedule set forth on Table 1.3 which is attached hereto and made a part hereof. No portion of the regulatory fee shall include a separate administration fee.
(b) The regulatory fee due hereunder shall be paid each calendar year. The regulatory fee shall be paid on an annual basis on January 1 of each year and shall be delinquent if not paid by March 1 of each year.
(c) Any new business, profession, practitioner or occupation required to pay a regulatory fee and which commences business in the county during a calendar year shall pay the full amount of the regulatory fee within ten days of beginning to transact business within the county.
(4) Multiple locations within the county. When a business, profession, practitioner or occupation conducts and/or transacts business at more than one fixed location or office within the unincorporated areas of the county, each location or office shall be considered a separate and distinct business, profession, practitioner or occupation for the purposes of payment of the regulatory fee required hereunder.
(Ord. No. 09-006, § V, 7-7-09)
Editor's note— The above-referenced Table 1.3 can be found in section 70-180 of this Code.

596:
(1) General exemptions. The following businesses, professions, practitioners and occupations are exempt from payment of the occupation tax and regulatory fees required by this article:
(a) Those businesses regulated by the Georgia Public Service Commission;
(b) Those electrical service businesses organized under Chapter 3 of Title 46 of the Official Code of Georgia Annotated;
(c) Any farm operation for the production from or on the land of agricultural products, but not including any agribusiness;
(d) Any disabled veteran of any war or armed conflict in which any branch of the armed forces of the United States engaged, whether under United States command or otherwise;
(e) Any blind person;
(f) Any veteran of peace-time service in the United States armed forces who has a physical disability incurred during the period of such service;
(g) Practitioners whose office is maintained by and who is employed in practice exclusively by the government of the United States, State of Georgia, or any county or any municipality of the State of Georgia;
(h) Any business operated solely for a charitable purpose. Provided, however, to qualify for said exemption a business must devote at least 50 percent of its gross receipts to charitable purposes and must furnish the county with written documentation confirming that at least 50 percent of its gross receipts were devoted to charitable purposes;
(i) Cooperative marketing associations governed by the provisions of O.C.G.A. § 2-10-105;
(j) Insurance companies governed by the provisions of O.C.G.A. § 33-8-8 et seq.;
(k) Motor common carriers governed by the provisions of O.C.G.A. § 46-7-15;
(l) Those businesses described in and governed by the provisions of O.C.G.A. § 48-5-355;
(m) Agricultural products and livestock raised in the state governed by the provisions of O.C.G.A. § 48-5-356;
(n) Depository financial institutions governed by the provisions of O.C.G.A. § 48-6-93;
(o) Facilities operated by a charitable trust governed by the provisions of O.C.G.A. § 48-13-55; and
(p) Any business, profession, practitioner or occupation upon which levy of an occupation tax or regulatory fee is prohibited by state or federal law.
(2) Exemption applicable to small businesses. Pursuant to the authority contained in the provisions of O.C.G.A. § 48-13-10(f), and in order to promote and encourage economic development and to further encourage, attract, develop and maintain small businesses within the county, including providing jobs and economic livelihood for many, all small businesses of whatever kind and nature located within the unincorporated areas of the county are exempt from paying the occupation tax imposed by this article. As utilized herein, the term "small business" shall be defined as a business or occupation which employs five or less employees. However, the term "small business" shall not include those professions or occupations listed on Table 1.2 hereof, regardless of the number of employees.
(3) Exemption applicable to real estate brokers. Pursuant to the requirements of O.C.G.A. § 48-13-17(a), no occupation tax shall be levied upon or collected from real estate brokers or agents who transact business within the county but whose offices are located outside the county. In the event the real estate agent or broker has a location or office within the county, he/she shall be subject to the occupation tax imposed hereunder.
(Ord. No. 09-006, § VI, 7-7-09)
Editor's note— The above-referenced Table 1.2 can be found in section 70-180 of this Code.

625:
(a) Prohibition. It shall be unlawful for any person to operate or conduct any motorcycle race on any motorcycle track or other places where such races would be held in the unincorporated areas of the county except as provided herein.
(b) Racing hours of operation. Motorcycle racing shall be permitted on the following days and during the following hours only: Saturday: 10:00 a.m. to 10:00 p.m.; Sunday: 1:00 p.m. to 6:00 p.m. Sanction races which shall be permitted on Friday, 10:00 a.m. to 10:00 p.m. and continue on Saturday and Sunday during the hours provided above.
(c) Motorcycle racing practice. Motorcycle racing practice shall be allowed on any two nights per week excluding Wednesday. Race practice shall be permitted from 3:30 p.m. to 9:00 p.m. on the two days per week selected for practice. Race practice shall also be permitted on the days and during the hours provided in (b) above.
(d) Outdoor commercial racing of motorcycles shall be subject to the following requirements:
(1) A special use permit shall be required as provided under the zoning ordinance;
(2) The operator shall obtain all necessary state and federal permits, if any, for motorcycle racing;
(3) A traffic impact study must be submitted with the application for a special use permit;
(4) No damaged motorcycles or other vehicles shall be stored on the site of the motorcycle raceway for more than seven days;
(5) A noise abatement plan and air pollution and dust abatement plan shall be submitted with the special use permit. The plan shall be approved by the planning commission as a condition to the issuance of the special use permit;
(6) An ambulance, provided at the expense of the race track operator shall be on station at each motorcycle race;
(7) A uniformed law enforcement officer, provided at the expenses of the race track operator, shall be present at each motorcycle race;
(8) The board of commissioners reserves the right to reconsider this article six months from the date of adoption or at any later time if the operation of motorcycle race tracks does not appear appropriate.
(Ord. of 2-21-89, § 2; Ord. of 7-27-00, § VII)

639:
A. The Catoosa County Comprehensive Plan is the official development policy and implementation guide for the county to coordinate and direct physical and economic development, related public investment, and, to provide reasonable regulations for the development of private property in the interest of public health, safety, convenience, and general welfare. This UDC is intended to implement all provisions of the Comprehensive Plan for the development and use of land.
B. The purpose of this UDC is to:
1) Provide for the security and stability of property owners;
2) Maintain property values;
3) Lessen congestion in the streets and roads;
4) Secure safety from fire, panic and other dangers;
5) Encourage economically sound land development;
6) Create a comprehensive and stable pattern of land uses upon which to plan public infrastructure and facilitate economic development;
7) Assure the provisions of required streets, utilities, and other facilities and services;
8) Assure the adequate provisions of safe and convenient traffic access and circulation, both vehicular and pedestrian;
9) Assure the provision of needed public open spaces and building sites through the dedication or reservation of land for recreational, educational, and other public purposes;
10) Assure the control and disposition of surface stormwater in new developments and adjacent areas;
11) Regulate the distribution and density of land uses relative to capacity of public infrastructure and to avoid both the undue concentration of population and the inappropriate dispersion of population;
12) Provide for the continuing orderly growth and development of the County;
13) Ensure that proposed development and new land uses protect natural resources and preserve the county's rural setting;
14) Prevent blight and deterioration while conserving the value of buildings;
15) Assure compatibility between different types of development and land uses;
16) Encourage the most appropriate use of land, buildings and structures; and
17) Assure that land is developed in conformity with the Catoosa County Comprehensive Plan.
